00:00:00.980 --> 00:00:03.999
Chris: So, what is meant by
Configuration Management?

00:00:05.370 --> 00:00:10.040
Configuration management is a process that
defines, controls, documents, and reports

00:00:10.250 --> 00:00:12.509
the ‘configuration items’ in a system.

00:00:13.370 --> 00:00:17.879
It was created to maintain consistency,
ensuring that the systems used to create

00:00:17.900 --> 00:00:23.760
the product, perform as they are expected to
as changes are made throughout, essentially

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:25.340
a way to manage a system configuration.

00:00:27.450 --> 00:00:31.560
Configuration management is a key part of
the ‘DevOps lifecycle’, and ‘Continuous

00:00:31.560 --> 00:00:38.580
Integration/Continuous Delivery’, or CI/CD
can be set up so change requests can be

00:00:38.610 --> 00:00:41.110
automatically deployed, to an existing system.

00:00:41.120 --> 00:00:47.170
Configuration management works well with the
‘Agile methodology’ as it supports ‘Agile

00:00:47.200 --> 00:00:50.199
teams’ to ‘prioritise’ configuration tasks.

00:00:51.679 --> 00:00:57.230
Leah: Software Configuration Management
or SCM is a process to manage, organise,

00:00:57.280 --> 00:01:01.989
and control the changes that occur in the
different aspects, such as code, documents,

00:01:01.990 --> 00:01:04.440
and other entities during a software project.

00:01:05.030 --> 00:01:11.899
Most large software projects would have a ‘SCM
co-ordinator’, to manage the process, as it

00:01:11.900 --> 00:01:13.390
could become quite complex given the number
of changes that would be made to the code.

00:01:13.390 --> 00:01:21.590
The ‘SCM co-ordinator’ would use
automated tools to support the process.

00:01:22.590 --> 00:01:28.380
Chris: Good SCM would ensure that all changes are
documented, consistent, compliant, and stable.

00:01:29.420 --> 00:01:34.050
A SCM system would define the system
settings; and would then build and maintain

00:01:34.050 --> 00:01:36.150
those systems according to the settings.

00:01:36.830 --> 00:01:40.170
It helps users know the current
state of a software product.

00:01:40.789 --> 00:01:44.370
This prevents issues so there are not as
many problems that could occur later in the

00:01:44.370 --> 00:01:49.330
project, such as code conflicts when there is
more than one Coder working on the same code.

00:01:49.910 --> 00:01:53.869
It also helps to ensure that the testing
and production environments match up

00:01:54.370 --> 00:01:58.800
and supports automation which would
reduce costs, complexity, and errors.

00:01:59.639 --> 00:02:05.309
Leah: A SCM system would have a set of software
tools which would be used to track and control

00:02:05.309 --> 00:02:07.880
changes in the stages of a software project.

00:02:08.289 --> 00:02:14.290
SCM tools are used to store the current state
of a system and help to maintain that state.

00:02:14.900 --> 00:02:19.009
They are used to support faster
changes and deployments and remove the

00:02:19.009 --> 00:02:23.569
potential for errors, making system
management, predictable and scalable.

00:02:24.199 --> 00:02:28.299
They also keep track of resources
and avoid task repetition.

00:02:29.420 --> 00:02:35.420
A SCM tool would improve a system recovery
with its automation capabilities, as

00:02:35.420 --> 00:02:39.540
it would also keep an audit trail which
would document any changes or updates.

00:02:40.459 --> 00:02:45.670
Therefore, if a system failed or
crashed, the reason could be identified

00:02:45.700 --> 00:02:47.870
and avoided in future deployments.

00:02:48.570 --> 00:02:54.679
Chris: Another configuration tool that is used,
is a Configuration Management Database or CMDB.

00:02:55.349 --> 00:02:58.230
This database helps to
manage configuration assets.

00:02:58.789 --> 00:03:03.010
It stores the configuration records for
the assets, and includes the attributes,

00:03:03.350 --> 00:03:05.590
such as type, owner, or version.

00:03:06.150 --> 00:03:09.730
It also stores the relationships
with other assets in the system.

00:03:10.440 --> 00:03:14.390
In a software project ‘SCM tools’
would be expected to include:

00:03:14.740 --> 00:03:19.730
• Asset management, the storing, classifying,
and managing of configuration assets.

00:03:20.450 --> 00:03:25.600
• Track changes, a version control system
would be used to track code changes.

00:03:26.340 --> 00:03:31.979
• Deployment, which would be automated from
the Continuous Delivery or ‘CD’ stage.

00:03:33.389 --> 00:03:36.599
• Configuration comparison, allowing
the current state to be compared

00:03:36.600 --> 00:03:38.620
to the previous state if needed.

00:03:39.720 --> 00:03:43.769
• Leah: Automated routine tasks, such
as running system patches and updates.

00:03:43.780 --> 00:03:48.350
• Configuration backup and recovery,
meaning the system can be restored

00:03:48.350 --> 00:03:50.370
from a backup If there are any issues.

00:03:50.990 --> 00:03:54.769
This would also be related to version
control and rollback strategy.

00:03:55.549 --> 00:04:00.810
• Visual dashboard, ensuring visibility and
ease of use for all members of the team,

00:04:01.350 --> 00:04:06.250
including the ‘Configuration co-ordinator’,
the ‘Developer team’, and the ‘Project manager’

00:04:06.799 --> 00:04:12.040
• Chris: Performance monitoring, identifying poor
performing and noncompliant configurations.

00:04:12.750 --> 00:04:17.060
• Troubleshooting, such as
identifying bugs or code conflicts.

00:04:17.829 --> 00:04:22.050
• Security, ensuring that the
system is secure and protected.

00:04:22.889 --> 00:04:28.599
• Analytics, so that reports can be generated
to resolve issues and for project planning.

00:04:29.799 --> 00:04:34.470
Leah: There are several ‘SCM tools’ on the
market today, that can be used, including open

00:04:34.470 --> 00:04:39.600
source and commercial brands, let’s look at
some examples and don’t forget you can check out

00:04:39.600 --> 00:04:41.770
our weblinks in this session to find out more:

00:04:41.770 --> 00:04:46.070
• Auvik - cloud-based Network
and Monitoring software

00:04:46.660 --> 00:04:48.910
• Ansible - IT Automation Framework

00:04:48.910 --> 00:04:54.490
• CFEngine - Configuration tool which
is both open source and commercial.

00:04:54.990 --> 00:04:57.530
• Chef - IT Automation Framework

00:04:58.000 --> 00:05:02.990
• Chris: Endpoint Central - Unified
Endpoint Management or UEM solution

00:05:03.679 --> 00:05:07.050
• Docker - Containerisation configuration management

00:05:07.790 --> 00:05:10.630
• Git – Distributed Version control system

00:05:11.480 --> 00:05:14.440
• Puppet - IT Automation Framework

00:05:15.110 --> 00:05:18.980
• Terraform - Configuration management
platform which is open source

00:05:20.630 --> 00:05:24.900
Leah: The tools that are selected for use,
would depend on the type of the software project

00:05:24.940 --> 00:05:29.929
and size of the team, and it is important to
remember that they would be part of a wider

00:05:29.940 --> 00:05:32.350
Software configuration management system.

00:05:32.800 --> 00:05:37.170
Therefore, in most cases there would
be more than one tool used such as

00:05:37.179 --> 00:05:39.090
‘version control’ and ‘deployment’.

